---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import ExponentAndLogCalculator from '@/components/tools/math/ExponentAndLogCalculator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="exponent-and-log-calculator">
  <ExponentAndLogCalculator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>Four related calculators</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Powers: raise any base to any exponent, including negative and fractional ones.</li>
      <li>Roots: square, cube and nth roots, including odd roots of negative numbers.</li>
      <li>Logarithms: any base, with the change-of-base rule, plus ln, log₁₀ and log₂.</li>
      <li>Scientific notation: convert in both directions, with engineering notation and significant figures.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Powers, roots and logs are three views of the same relation. If bⁿ = x, then the nth root of x is b and
      log base b of x is n. Each tab solves for a different unknown.
    </p>

    <h2>Exponent rules</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Statement</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Product</td><td>bᵐ × bⁿ = bᵐ⁺ⁿ</td><td>2³ × 2⁴ = 2⁷ = 128</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quotient</td><td>bᵐ ÷ bⁿ = bᵐ⁻ⁿ</td><td>2⁷ ÷ 2⁴ = 2³ = 8</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Power of a power</td><td>(bᵐ)ⁿ = bᵐⁿ</td><td>(2³)² = 2⁶ = 64</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Zero exponent</td><td>b⁰ = 1 for b ≠ 0</td><td>7⁰ = 1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Negative exponent</td><td>b⁻ⁿ = 1 ÷ bⁿ</td><td>2⁻³ = 1/8 = 0.125</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fractional exponent</td><td>b^(m/n) = ⁿ√(bᵐ)</td><td>8^(2/3) = (∛8)² = 4</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The negative and fractional rules follow from the product rule; they are not arbitrary conventions. If
      b³ × b⁻³ must equal b⁰ = 1, then b⁻³ can only be the reciprocal of b³.
    </p>

    <h2>Logarithm rules</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rule</th><th scope="col">Statement</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Product</td><td>log(xy) = log x + log y</td><td>log₁₀(2 × 50) = 0.301 + 1.699 = 2</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Quotient</td><td>log(x ÷ y) = log x − log y</td><td>log₂(32 ÷ 4) = 5 − 2 = 3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Power</td><td>log(xⁿ) = n · log x</td><td>log₁₀(10³) = 3 × 1 = 3</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Change of base</td><td>log_b(x) = ln x ÷ ln b</td><td>log₂(1024) = 6.931 ÷ 0.693 = 10</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Identity</td><td>log_b(b) = 1 and log_b(1) = 0</td><td>log₇(7) = 1</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Logarithms were invented in the early 1600s to turn multiplication into addition, which is also how slide
      rules work. With the change-of-base rule, this calculator handles a base of 3, 7 or 1.05 without a separate
      function for each.
    </p>

    <h2>Why some inputs are rejected</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Log of zero or a negative number: no power of a positive base gives zero or a negative result, so there is no real answer. As x approaches zero from above, log x goes to −∞.</li>
      <li>Base 1 logarithms: 1 to any power is 1, so the question “what power of 1 gives 8?” has no answer.</li>
      <li>Even roots of negatives: any real number squared is non-negative, so √(−4) is not real. Odd roots work: ∛(−27) = −3.</li>
      <li>Zero to a negative power: that is 1 ÷ 0, which is undefined.</li>
      <li>Negative base with a fractional exponent: (−8)^0.5 would be an even root of a negative number.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Scientific notation</h2>
    <p>
      Scientific notation writes a number as a coefficient between 1 and 10, multiplied by a power of ten. Move
      the decimal point until one non-zero digit is in front of it, and count the moves: moves to the left give a
      positive exponent, moves to the right a negative one.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Number</th><th scope="col">Scientific</th><th scope="col">Engineering</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>149,600,000</td><td>1.496 × 10⁸</td><td>149.6 × 10⁶</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0.000 000 45</td><td>4.5 × 10⁻⁷</td><td>450 × 10⁻⁹</td></tr>
          <tr><td>602,214,076,000,000,000,000,000</td><td>6.02214076 × 10²³</td><td>602.214076 × 10²¹</td></tr>
          <tr><td>0.0025</td><td>2.5 × 10⁻³</td><td>2.5 × 10⁻³</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Engineering notation uses only exponents that are multiples of three, so it matches the SI prefixes (kilo,
      mega, giga, milli, micro, nano). For this reason electronics and mechanical work prefer it.
    </p>

    <h2>Where logs show up in real units</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Decibels: 10·log₁₀ of a power ratio. Ten decibels is a tenfold change in power.</li>
      <li>pH: the negative base-10 log of hydrogen ion concentration, so pH 4 is ten times more acidic than pH 5.</li>
      <li>Richter magnitude: base 10, so a magnitude 6 releases about 32 times the energy of a magnitude 5.</li>
      <li>Algorithmic complexity: binary search takes log₂(n) steps, so twice the data adds only one step.</li>
      <li>Compound growth: the time to reach a target is ln(target ÷ start) ÷ ln(1 + rate).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Precision</h2>
    <p>
      Calculations use double-precision floating point, about 15 significant digits, and results show to ten
      decimal places without trailing zeros. Very large or very small results change automatically to exponential
      form. Powers larger than about 1.8 × 10³⁰⁸ are too large for a double, and the tool says so and does not show
      “Infinity”. All calculations run in your browser, and nothing is uploaded.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
